---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import InteriorHero from '../../components/InteriorHero.astro';
import SectionShell from '../../components/SectionShell.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Residential Epoxy Flooring Edmonton | Final Finish" description="Plan a residential epoxy floor for Edmonton garages, barns and workshops with preparation, finish and care questions that belong in the quote." canonicalPath="/services/residential-epoxy-flooring">
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main-content">
    <InteriorHero eyebrow="Garages · Barns · Workshops" title="Residential epoxy flooring for the spaces you live with." lead="Turn worn concrete into a brighter, more considered part of the home with flake, solid or metallic finish options—after the slab and daily use have been discussed." />
    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">Start with the actual floor</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">What we assess before choosing a finish</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">A site conversation starts with the concrete, not a colour chip. Bring up cracks, patches, low areas, old coatings, oil or de-icing residue, water history and the routes that cars, tools, pets or stored items use. Those observations help define what preparation and repairs belong in the scope.</p>
      <p>Photos are a useful beginning, but the written quote should identify the planned preparation, visible repair allowance or change process, finish area, edges and access assumptions. If a condition cannot be confirmed until preparation begins, it is more useful to name that uncertainty than to turn it into a promise. The <a href="/resources/garage-floor-preparation-edmonton">garage preparation guide</a> and <a href="/resources/floor-coating-site-assessment-checklist">site-assessment checklist</a> show what to record before a visit.</p>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">Finish is a use decision</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Choose the look around how the room is used</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Flake</h3><p>Consider flake when you want a patterned field that can make everyday grit less visually prominent. Review a physical sample beside cabinets, trim and lighting; the <a href="/resources/floor-flake-colour-design-guide">flake and colour guide</a> gives a practical sample process.</p></article>
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Solid colour</h3><p>A solid finish makes seams, repairs, texture and cleanability questions easier to discuss directly. Ask how the proposed texture suits footwear, water, slopes and the cleaning method—not whether it is universally “non-slip.”</p></article>
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Metallic</h3><p>Metallic effects are visual compositions rather than a standard pattern. Use the <a href="/resources/metallic-epoxy-expectations">metallic expectations guide</a> to discuss samples, boundaries and the parts of the floor likely to be covered.</p></article>
      </div>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">Winter and ordinary care</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Plan for salt, meltwater and cleaning before the first vehicle returns</h2>
      <p>In an Edmonton garage, tracked grit, de-icing residue and meltwater deserve a routine, not an assumption that the finish removes every maintenance task. Ask which cleaner, dilution, tools, mats and drying method are permitted for the exact installed system, and keep those instructions with the project record. The <a href="/resources/winter-salt-concrete-floor-damage">winter-salt concrete guide</a> explains why residue and water history should be part of the conversation; the <a href="/resources/how-to-clean-coated-garage-floor">garage cleaning guide</a> helps frame the questions for handover.</p>
      <p>If a tire leaves a mark or a coating appears to lift, pause before escalating cleaning or grinding. The <a href="/resources/hot-tire-pickup-epoxy">hot-tire pickup guide</a> distinguishes a symptom worth documenting from a diagnosis.</p>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">Make quotes comparable</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A useful quote names the assumptions as well as the finish</h2>
      <p>Ask what is included for preparation, crack or spall repair, moving stored items, perimeter details, moisture investigation when relevant, selected system layers, return-to-use restrictions and cleanup. Compare the same scope across proposals; chemistry labels alone do not describe a complete floor. Use the <a href="/resources/how-to-compare-floor-coating-proposals">proposal comparison guide</a> and <a href="/resources/questions-to-ask-floor-coating-contractor">contractor question list</a>, then confirm the final system and restrictions in writing.</p>
      <div class="button-row"><a class="button button-primary" href="/floor-visualizer">Try a finish</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/contact#quote">Request a residential quote</a></div>
    </SectionShell>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
